Scenario
You are deploying a hardened production application named secure-app. Following security best practices, the application container has been configured to run as a non-privileged user (User ID 1000), rather than root.

However, the deployment is failing. The application crashes immediately upon startup because it cannot write to its persistent storage volume.

Your task is to diagnose the permission conflict and perform a "surgical fix" to the volume permissions using a temporary container, allowing the secure application to run successfully.

Task
Working directory: /home/user/adv-lab-1/

1. Infrastructure Prep:

Create a Named Volume called app-storage.
2. The Failure Analysis:

Attempt to run a container named secure-app using the image alpine.

Configuration:

Mount app-storage to /data.

Force the container to run as User ID 1000 (simulate a non-root image).

Command: sh -c "echo 'Secure Log' > /data/app.log && sleep 3600"

Observation: The container will likely fail or exit immediately. Use docker logs or inspect to confirm the "Permission Denied" error.

3. The Fix (The Core Task):

You cannot change the secure-app configuration (it must run as UID 1000).
You cannot run sudo commands on the host machine to change directory permissions.
Action: Launch a temporary "fixer" container (running as root) that mounts the same volume. Use this container to change the ownership of the /data directory inside the volume to UID 1000.
4. Verification:

Start the secure-app container again in the background (as UID 1000).
Verify it stays running.
Verify the file /data/app.log is successfully created.
Expected Outcome
secure-app is currently running.
docker ps shows the container is active.
The volume app-storage contains app.log.

COMMAND:-

cd adv-lab-1/
    3  docker volume create app-storage
    4  docker run --name secure-app   --user 1000   -v app-storage:/data   alpine   sh -c "echo 'Secure Log' > /data/app.log && sleep 3600"
    5  docker logs secure-app
    6  docker run --rm   -v app-storage:/data   alpine   sh -c "chown -R 1000:1000 /data"
    7  docker rm secure-app
    8  docker run -d --name secure-app   --user 1000   -v app-storage:/data   alpine   sh -c "echo 'Secure Log' > /data/app.log && sleep 3600"
    9  docker ps
   10  docker exec -it secure-app ls /data
   11  docker exec -it secure-app cat /data/app.log
   

